---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { ABOUT_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE, YOUTUBE_CHANNEL } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About ${SITE_TITLE} | Independent Creative Studio`}
			description={ABOUT_DESCRIPTION}
		/>
	</head>
	<body class="about-page">
		<a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
		<SiteHeader />
		<main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
			<header class="about-page__intro">
				<p class="eyebrow">About Petalwake</p>
				<h1>Stories matter<br /><em>because people do.</em></h1>
				<p class="about-page__lead">
					Petalwake Studios is an independent, evangelical Christian creative studio exploring the
					beauty, complexity, and wonder of the human experience through film, animation, and
					original audiovisual work.
				</p>
				<p>
					We create stories inspired by the things that shape us: love, loss, memory, forgiveness,
					hope, and the quiet moments that give life meaning. Through cinematic imagery, atmosphere,
					music, and visual storytelling, we seek to tell stories that linger beyond the screen.
				</p>
			</header>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--light" aria-labelledby="foundation-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Our foundation</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="foundation-title">Evangelical Christian faith, expressed in creativity.</h2>
					<p>
						Petalwake is openly evangelical Christian. We believe the gospel is the good news of
						God's love, the brokenness of humanity, and the grace of Jesus Christ. That conviction
						shapes how we tell stories, with honesty about suffering, moral responsibility, and the
						possibility of forgiveness and hope.
					</p>
					<p>
						Our work is not defined by a single formula or by forcing every story to be a direct
						religious lesson. Instead, we aim to tell stories that are honest, human, and careful,
						letting a Christian understanding of grace, sin, and redemption inform the questions we
						ask without reducing every story to a sermon.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--light" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Our approach</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="approach-title">Image, sound, and meaning</h2>
					<p>
						A story can communicate through more than dialogue alone. A landscape, a musical
						phrase, a lingering glance, or the silence between two people can express something
						that words cannot fully capture. We are interested in the relationship between image,
						sound, emotion, and meaning—and in how they can work together to create a memorable
						experience.
					</p>
					<p>
						Each project has its own needs and visual identity. Some stories may be animated;
						others may explore different creative forms. What connects them is an interest in
						storytelling that invites an audience to pause, reflect, and see familiar things from
						a new perspective.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section" aria-labelledby="why-stories-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Why stories</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="why-stories-title">A life beyond our own</h2>
					<p>
						A story can take us somewhere we have never been, place us inside a life we have never
						lived, and ask us to see another person with greater honesty and compassion. That is
						what draws us to animation and film: the chance to sit with people who are imperfect,
						wounded, hopeful, frightened, loving, and capable of change.
					</p>
					<p>
						We are interested in what happens when people fail one another, when grief changes the
						shape of a life, and when forgiveness becomes costly. Not every question has a simple
						answer. We begin with character, image, atmosphere, and the choices people make—not
						with a lesson to deliver.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--light" aria-labelledby="beliefs-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">What we believe about stories</p>
				<div class="about-principles">
					<h2 id="beliefs-title">Honest stories leave room.</h2>
					<div class="about-principles__grid">
						<article>
							<h3>People are worth understanding</h3>
							<p>
								Characters are more than heroes or villains. Understanding how someone became
								who they are can deepen our sense of their choices and responsibilities; it
								does not make the harm they cause disappear. Stories can offer context without
								asking an audience to excuse harm. We want characters to be met with curiosity
								and care, while keeping the effects of their actions visible to the people around them.
							</p>
						</article>
						<article>
							<h3>Pain deserves honesty</h3>
							<p>
								We do not want to hurry suffering toward a convenient resolution. Some losses
								cannot be undone, some trust cannot simply be restored, and some choices leave
								consequences that remain part of a life. Making room for pain means allowing
								difficult feelings and consequences to remain present. A story need not turn
								every wound into a lesson or promise that time alone will put things right.
							</p>
						</article>
						<article>
							<h3>Hope can be honest, too</h3>
							<p>
								Hope does not require denying grief or overlooking wrongdoing. It may begin
								quietly, while a difficult season is still here, without explaining away every
								painful thing that came before. We are drawn to hopeful moments that feel
								earned by what came before. They may offer no guarantee about what happens next,
								only a reason to keep looking for possibility alongside grief.
							</p>
						</article>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section" aria-labelledby="wonder-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Stories that awaken wonder</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="wonder-title">More than spectacle</h2>
					<p>
						Wonder can live in an imagined world or a beautiful landscape. It can also arrive in
						an ordinary kindness, a difficult truth finally spoken, or the recognition that
						another person is more than their worst moment. We want to notice beauty without
						looking away from suffering, and to stay open to mystery without pretending that
						every question is easy.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--light" aria-labelledby="connection-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Connection and conversation</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="connection-title">Stories become places to meet</h2>
					<p>
						Some of the best stories do not end when the credits appear. They become conversations.
						Our hope is that our work can bring people into contact with a character, an experience
						outside their own, another perspective, or a difficult question—and sometimes with one
						another. We hope our stories create space for reflection, conversation, connection,
						and, sometimes, healing.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--inspiration" aria-labelledby="inspiration-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Our inspiration</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="inspiration-title">Beauty that points beyond itself</h2>
					<p>
						The natural world has a remarkable capacity to inspire wonder. For us, the beauty of
						creation can point beyond itself, inviting contemplation of the One who made it.
					</p>
					<blockquote>
						<p>
							“The heavens declare the glory of God; and the firmament sheweth his handywork.”
						</p>
						<cite>Psalm 19:1 (King James Version)</cite>
					</blockquote>
					<p>
						This passage is an important inspiration for Petalwake’s creative direction. It
						informs our appreciation for beauty and the natural world, and our belief that art
						can encourage reflection on questions larger than ourselves. It is an inspiration
						for the studio, not a claim that every project is explicitly religious.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="about-page__section about-page__section--closing" aria-labelledby="looking-forward-title">
				<p class="eyebrow">Looking forward</p>
				<div>
					<h2 id="looking-forward-title">We’re just beginning</h2>
					<p>
						Petalwake Studios is being built with a long horizon: a home for original characters,
						imagined worlds, films, and stories that have something worth saying. We are growing
						toward a larger creative vision one story at a time, beginning with the work in front
						of us and leaving room for what is still to come. We explore visual storytelling,
						music, animation, and emerging creative tools while keeping the story and intended
						audience experience at the center of each project.
					</p>
					<p><a href="/work/">Explore Petalwake Films <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
					<p><a href={YOUTUBE_CHANNEL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit our YouTube channel <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter />
	</body>
</html>
